//? Title: Jump-and-walk: locate a point by actual edge crossings
//? Subtitle: Exact turns, sampled starts and distinct geometric versus budget outcomes
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Which triangle contains a query point? Jump-and-walk samples a promising start,
then crosses edges whose far side contains the query. We begin with a
two-triangle square, retain the original sixty-point random cloud and four
queries, and inspect the producing native sample and edge-test histories. Exact
orientation signs handle boundary and extreme-coordinate cases. Work
measurements separate sampling from walking; exhausted budgets remain distinct
from exterior location, and distribution-dependent theory is separated from this
implementation's heuristic.
)

\toc

> Cross one actual edge first

Split the square with corners (0,0), (4,0), (4,4), (0,4) along its rising
diagonal. Face 0 is (0,1,2); face 1 is (0,2,3). For query (3,1), seed 1 samples
face 1 twice. The query is right of its directed diagonal 0 to 2, so the walk
crosses to face 0. Three nonnegative tests then certify containment. The native
diagram reads that negative test and its actual neighbor; its arrows show
decision flow rather than a geometric segment route.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/jump_walk_crossing.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const std::vector<dm::geometry::Point2D> p = {{0, 0}, {4, 0}, {4, 4}, {0, 4}};
    const std::vector<dm::geometry::Triangle> f = {{0, 1, 2}, {0, 2, 3}};
    const auto t = dm::geometry::TriangulationLocator(p, f).trace({3, 1}, 1);
    std::print("| sample | face | normalized score | best face |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.samples.size(); ++i) {
        const auto &s = t.samples[i];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {:.12f} | {} |\n", i, s.triangle, s.score,
                   s.best_triangle);
    }
    std::print("\n| step | face | first edge |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.steps.size(); ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", i, t.steps[i].triangle,
                   t.steps[i].first_edge);
    std::print("\n| step | face | edge | u | v | exact sign | neighbor |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | " "---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (const auto &e : t.tests)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", e.step, e.triangle,
                   e.edge, e.u, e.v, e.sign, e.neighbor);
    std::print("\nThe actual result is face {}, after {} crossings and {} exact edge tests. Neighbor -2 " "means no lookup was needed.\n",
               t.analysis.triangle, t.analysis.work.crossings,
               t.analysis.work.edge_tests);
    dm::geometry::jump_walk_diagram(t).save_svg("build/jump_walk_crossing.svg");
}
```
