> Derive containment and the crossing rule

For a counterclockwise triangle, an interior or boundary query lies on the left
of every directed edge. Its sign test is

$$
D(u,v,q)=(v_x-u_x)(q_y-u_y)-(v_y-u_y)(q_x-u_x).
$$
\alttext(The orientation determinant is the cross product of edge displacement and query displacement.)

If all three signs are nonnegative, q is in the closed triangle. If a sign is
negative, the containing triangle, if it exists in a conforming convex-domain
mesh, lies on the other side of that supporting line. The native kernel crosses
the adjacent face of that edge. A missing neighbor is a boundary exit. No test
uses a tolerance: the canonical predicate computes the exact sign of the
represented finite binary64 coordinates, including subnormals. This is not an
uncertainty model for unrounded measurements.

The centroid score chooses only a start. The kernel samples ceil(cuberoot(T))
original faces with replacement, with T the triangle count. It keeps the first
strict minimum score. Coordinates share a common max(1,absolute mesh/query
coordinate) scale before centroid and squared-distance arithmetic, avoiding
overflow; long-double rounding or underflow can still tie scores. These
heuristic scores never decide containment. The legacy xorshift64 stream,
zero-seed fallback and modulo mapping remain; each entered face uses a random
cyclic edge start, not a uniform permutation of six orders.

Crossing a separating edge does not imply that triangle centroids move
monotonically closer to q. A fixed pseudorandom stream also does not establish
an almost-sure termination theorem. The implementation therefore has an explicit
walk bound and reports its exhaustion separately.

> Retain the original sixty-point cloud

The fixture keeps the original mt19937 seed 7, uniform[0,100) distribution,
sixty points and draw order. Standard-library distribution mapping can affect
exact coordinates. The current canonical Delaunay triangulator supplies strict
counterclockwise faces and stable face ordering; the old cached face numbers are
not used as evidence. The four original queries remain (50,50), (20,80), (75,30)
and (−10,−10). The plot joins centroids of the actually entered faces: these
connectors visualize visit order, not a straight-line tracing algorithm. The
measured centroid distances also supply a concrete counterexample to monotone
centroid descent in this original run.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/jump_walk_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const auto p = dm::geometry::jump_walk_demo_points();
    const auto f = dm::geometry::delaunay_triangulation(p);
    const dm::geometry::TriangulationLocator loc(p, f);
    std::print("The original cloud contains {} points and {} triangles.\n\n",
               p.size(), f.size());
    std::print("| query x | query y | start face | result face | samples | entered faces | exact tests | " "crossings |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (const auto q : std::vector<dm::geometry::Point2D>{{50, 50}, {20, 80},
                                                           {75, 30}, {-10, -10}}) {
        const auto a = loc.analyze(q, 1);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", q.x, q.y,
                   a.start_triangle, a.triangle, a.work.samples, a.work.steps,
                   a.work.edge_tests, a.work.crossings);
    }
    const auto t = loc.trace({50, 50}, 1);
    std::print("\n| actual step | face | cyclic start edge | centroid distance to query |\n| ---: | ---: | " "---: | ---: |\n");
    double previous = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
    std::size_t increases = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.steps.size(); ++i) {
        const auto &face = f[t.steps[i].triangle];
        const double cx = p[face.a].x / 3 + p[face.b].x / 3 + p[face.c].x / 3;
        const double cy = p[face.a].y / 3 + p[face.b].y / 3 + p[face.c].y / 3;
        const double distance = std::hypot(cx - 50, cy - 50);
        increases += distance > previous;
        previous = distance;
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.6f} |\n", i, t.steps[i].triangle,
                   t.steps[i].first_edge, distance);
    }
    std::print("\nThe actual centroid distance increases on {} transition; edge crossing is not " "centroid-distance descent.\n",
               increases);
    dm::geometry::jump_walk_plot(t).title("Original cloud: actual entered-face route").save_svg("build/jump_walk_original.svg");
}
```
