// result_end
\caption(Query sampling and mean exact edge tests over all thirty-two seeds.)
\alttext(The curves show sample counts and the average number of actual exact orientation tests on five increasingly fine regular grids; logarithmic axes do not establish a rate theorem.)

Building cached adjacency takes expected O(P+T) hash work and O(T) storage for P
points and T faces. An untraced query uses O(S+W) expected hash work and
constant auxiliary storage for S samples and W started walk steps, with at most
three exact edge tests per step. The default finite bound is 4T+16 steps; its
worst-case allowance is linear in T. One-shot locate_triangle() repeats
construction, whereas a borrowed TriangulationLocator amortizes it across
queries. Owned tracing adds O(P+T+K) copy work and storage for K
sample/start/test records.

The often-quoted expected cube-root result concerns random-point Delaunay models
and a particular sampled-start/straight-march analysis. This code instead
samples triangle centroids with replacement and uses a cyclic visibility walk.
Its implementation and the measured grids therefore do not inherit that theorem
automatically. The primary papers below explain the different assumptions and
walk strategies.

> Lifetimes, limits and evidence

A locator borrows both input vectors: they must outlive it and remain unchanged,
including through its public pointer/map fields. Const queries have independent
local RNG/work state. A returned trace owns the complete original mesh and
actual scalar histories, so it survives destruction of the locator and inputs.
Samples precede steps; each edge-test record names its actual step and directed
endpoints. There are no repeated-prefix runs or reconstructed search decisions.

Default construction admits one million complete P+T records after
finite/index/orientation checks and before adjacency allocation. Zero disables
that optional ceiling. Trace defaults admit one million actual sample/start/test
records online, before each operation; zero disables the optional trace ceiling,
and untraced analysis ignores it. Failure throws without exposing a partial
trace. A positive max_steps overrides the default; zero selects the default
rather than an unbounded walk. Record ceilings exclude vector capacity, byte
counts, allocator overhead and peak RAM. Plotting has a separate
complete-display ceiling and finite padded-span restriction; the exact locator
accepts extreme finite coordinates that cannot be plotted with representable
padded spans.

Fifteen targeted sanitizer and native tests cover grid boundaries,
original/random queries, exact subnormal/extreme triangles, invalid topology,
packed-index guards, caps, ownership and formatting. An independent rational
oracle checks 2,682 complete queries, 11,616 samples, 10,212 entered faces and
21,505 exact edge tests, including RNG chronology, adjacency, complete
containment scans and the explicit budget stop. A frozen old-kernel comparison
preserves triangle answers and all 51,937 tested-edge operand triples on 5,004
ordinary original-mesh queries. The subnormal exterior control corrects a
rounded-product false containment. No external dataset is needed for this
geometric experiment. The analysis shows what the actual sampled visibility walk
did, while keeping correctness certificates, resource limits and distributional
performance claims separate.

> References

Ernst P. Mücke, Isaac Saias and Binhai Zhu (1999), “Fast randomized point
location without preprocessing in two- and three-dimensional Delaunay
triangulations”, Computational Geometry 12(1–2), 63–83,
[doi:10.1016/S0925-7721(98)00035-2|https://doi.org/10.1016/S0925-7721(98)00035-2],
[author paper, especially pages 64–65|https://web.cs.ucdavis.edu/~amenta/w07/jump.and.walk.pdf].
Their analyzed jump-and-march samples vertices without replacement and traverses
a segment; this report's centroid visibility variant is specified above.

Olivier Devillers, Sylvain Pion and Monique Teillaud,
[Walking in a triangulation, INRIA research report 4120 (2001)|https://www.wias-berlin.de/people/si/course/files/DevillersPion-Walking.pdf],
distinguishes straight, visibility and stochastic strategies. Randomized
termination statements require their assumptions; a bounded deterministic
pseudorandom implementation still exposes an explicit exhaustion outcome.
